    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left&lt;2,3\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left&lt;2,6\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>t</m>, obtaining
<me>x =  y^{2/3}.</me>
This gives us <m>x</m> as a function of <m>y</m>, and we notice that <m>x</m> is always positive, so the correct graph is A.



We calculate the velocity and acceleration by taking derivatives:
<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
\mathbf{v}(t) \amp =\amp  \left\lt 2t,3t^{2}\right&gt; \\
\mathbf{a}(t) \amp =\amp  \left\lt 2,6t\right&gt;
\end{array}</me>
and then plug in <m>t=1</m> to obtain
<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
\mathbf{v}(1) \amp =\amp  \left\lt 2,3\right&gt; \\
\mathbf{a}(1) \amp =\amp  \left\lt 2,6\right&gt;
\end{array}</me>



It remains to determine the direction of motion. Since the graph is <m>x</m> as a function of <m>y</m>, we know the direction is either "Bottom-to-Top" or "Top-to-Bottom." Since <m>\frac{d y}{dt}=3t^{2}</m> is always positive, <m>y</m> is always increasing and we determine that the direction is "Bottom-to-Top".


</solution>
